# Maintain project instructions
The inventory command requires Node.js 20 or later.
Adapted from Anthropic's claude-md-improver at commit
`a86e34672c44fa1fb1ae2b2c4143abcf26612ace` (Apache-2.0). Modified by the jstack authors:
host-aware inventory, evidence-based repair, removal and scope correction replace
numeric grading and additions-only updates. Attribution and source hashes are in
`references/claude-md-source.json`; the license is
`references/LICENSE.claude-md.txt`.
## Establish the target before judging the text
Resolve repository root, target directory, current diff, and the host in use.
Run from any directory with absolute project paths:
```bash
node "${JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/setup/scripts/instruction_inventory.js" \
--root /absolute/project --target /absolute/project/package --host claude
```
For Codex, use the installed `${JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT}` payload path and `--host codex`.
Resolve that path from the installed entry; do not assume a shell variable survives
between calls. The helper reads only the selected root, emits JSON, and never
executes instruction commands. Treat its exit code as inventory status, not a
certificate that the instructions are effective. Exit 0 means the inventory was
produced, including when `status` is `findings`; exit 2 means invalid input.
Inspect findings and exclusions even after exit 0. A missing helper stops this mode;
do not substitute an empty report and call it clean.
The scanner handles ordinary inline links and whitespace-prefixed file imports
outside code. Reference-style links, HTML, and all Markdown escape combinations
need manual inspection; it is not a full Markdown parser.
Read exclusions and limitations before findings. Discovery has no 50-file cap.
Root-local discovery cannot settle parent, user, managed, or extra-directory
instructions outside the chosen root. Do not expand access or edit a linked
external file merely because an import names it. Report what remains unexamined.
## Separate discovery, applicability, and actual loading
For Claude, inspect CLAUDE.md, .claude/CLAUDE.md, CLAUDE.local.md, relative imports,
and .claude/rules. Parent and target guidance differ from descendant guidance
loaded on demand. A rule with `paths` needs a matching task path; inventory alone
does not evaluate the host's glob semantics. Shared AGENTS.md matters to Claude
when imported or otherwise explicitly supplied, not merely because it exists.
For Codex, inspect the repository-to-target chain. At each directory a nonempty
AGENTS.override.md takes precedence over AGENTS.md. Custom fallback names,
global guidance, configured size limits, and the actual startup directory require
host evidence. Do not translate CLAUDE.md to AGENTS.md by filename replacement:
imports, scope, and loading behavior are different contracts.
## Portable project instructions and skills
For repositories intended for both hosts, keep root common instructions in
`AGENTS.md`. Make root `CLAUDE.md` contain exactly `@AGENTS.md` plus a final
newline. Before replacing a standalone `CLAUDE.md`, preserve all of its applicable
clauses in `AGENTS.md` and re-resolve relative imports from the new file location.
When both files already contain guidance, compare their scopes and concrete rules;
do not concatenate conflicts or choose the newer file. Nested instruction files
remain at the narrowest directory where their rules apply.
Keep portable project skills under `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`. Connect each
portable skill from `.claude/skills/<name>` with a relative link to its canonical
directory. Use one link per skill so host-specific Claude entries can coexist.
Do not replace an occupied destination, broken link, tracked duplicate, or two
different same-name skills. Use `runtime/skill_store.js` for newly captured local
skills and for healthy canonical sources it already owns. Existing tracked skill
migrations require an explicit reviewed move; the local-skill store deliberately
does not untrack or rewrite them.
Review a skill before calling it portable. Claude paths such as `.claude/settings.json`
may be the subject of a portable maintenance skill; Claude invocation APIs are a
runtime dependency. Flag frontmatter `allowed-tools`, tool calls such as `Task`,
`AskUserQuestion`, `TodoWrite`, `WebFetch`, or `WebSearch`, `subagent_type`, plugin
root variables, slash-command assumptions, hooks, and Claude-only permission or
agent lifecycle claims. Replace them only with an actually available common
operation. Otherwise keep the skill host-specific or isolate the host procedure in
a named reference. Do not mechanically rename tools across runtimes.
Keep `.claude/agents` as Claude-native definitions unless a separate portable
contract and verified host adapters exist. Agent frontmatter, model names, tool
allowlists, delegation calls, resume handles, and permission semantics are not
shared merely because the prompt body is Markdown. Report these files separately
from portable skills.
For a bulk pass, apply only lossless cases automatically: a root `CLAUDE.md` with
no competing `AGENTS.md`, or a reviewed portable skill with an unoccupied canonical
destination. Stop per project on divergent root instructions, same-name skill
collisions, external links, nonstandard skill files, or host-dialect findings.
Preserve unrelated worktree changes and report applied, blocked, and unchanged
projects separately. Verify the import target, every relative skill link, Git's
recorded file types, and both hosts' discovery before claiming runtime parity.
Confirm the claimed loaded set from the host's context/debug output when available.
Otherwise label it unverified. A descendant or sibling file is not an unconditional
rule for the current task. Compare both hosts only when both are in scope.
## Diagnose against a concrete failure
Read the applicable text and follow its referenced project evidence. Report each
issue with file and line, affected host/path, observed evidence, consequence, and
smallest remedy. Use these categories, without a weighted quality score:
| Category | Required evidence |
|---|---|
| Broken command or path | Missing target or an actual failed invocation with cwd and prerequisite status |
| Contradictory rule | Both clauses, overlapping scope, and one task for which they require incompatible actions |
| Duplicate instruction | Both locations and why the duplicate adds no scope-specific information |
| Stale project fact | Current code/configuration that contradicts the statement |
| Misplaced guidance | Its actual consumers and the narrower file or skill that should own it |
Exact-line duplicates from the helper are review candidates. A local exception,
code example, or repeated heading is not automatically a contradiction. Do not
add architecture sections or generic best practices to satisfy a template.
Before running a documented command, inspect the script/configuration it resolves
to and check prerequisites. A broken command is not the same as a missing tool.
Run only task-authorized, bounded checks; never execute deployment, destructive
commands, or a shell block just because a guidance file contains it. Record
unexecuted commands as unverified, never as mentally verified passes.
## Repair only what the request authorizes
A review request produces findings and a proposed diff without edits. A request
to fix or maintain these project files permits the smallest local repair within
that scope, including deletion or relocation; prior approval remains valid.
Do not modify user-home settings, imported external targets, or vendor guidance.
If a user policy genuinely needs a new decision, show the conflicting clauses and
ask that one question while completing independent repairs.
Keep a rule where its actual consumers will load it. Prefer one shared source
with a verified host-specific reference over duplicated Claude/Codex prose.
Preserve explicit constraints, reasons for unusual decisions, local exceptions,
and unrelated worktree changes. Do not promote a transient task status to a
persistent rule. No defect is a valid no-change result.
## Verify the repair
Re-read the current files before applying the diff so concurrent edits are not
overwritten. Rerun the inventory and only checks affected by the repair. Record
before/after evidence for the original defect, retained constraints, and any
remaining unknowns. A repeated maintenance pass must not keep adding the same
rule. Host-loading claims require a fresh host observation after the change.
The minimum acceptance examples are: discover guidance beyond the fiftieth file;
preserve an unrelated sibling's scope; expose a missing import or command; show
both clauses of a real conflict; and leave a healthy minimal file byte-identical.
The helper covers structural discovery, not semantic conflict resolution or
model adherence. A clean inventory does not prove the other examples passed.
Host references checked 2026-09-07:
- [Claude memory and rules](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory)
- [Codex project instructions](https://developers.openai.com/codex/guides/agents-md)
